\section{Background and Problem Setup}
\label{sec:background}

We formalize compound sparsity, define the Interaction Index, and introduce phase segmentation. Full mathematical details appear in Appendix~\ref{app:formulation-details}.

\paragraph{Compound sparsity.}
Let $s \in (0,1)$ denote a unified sparsity budget. \emph{Attention head pruning} removes the bottom-$s$ fraction of heads per layer by Frobenius norm importance \cite{voita-acl2019, michel-neurips2019}. \emph{KV cache eviction} removes the bottom-$s$ fraction of token positions from the cache. We compare two strategies: \emph{importance-based}, which scores positions by position-normalized cumulative attention mass (Eq.~\ref{eq:kv-importance} in Appendix~\ref{app:formulation-details}), and \emph{random}, which evicts uniformly at random \cite{nacl-acl2024}. Applying both simultaneously yields \emph{compound sparsity}; the two axes interact because head pruning alters the importance ranking used for eviction \cite{spark-aaai2026}. Existing joint-pruning approaches such as GRASPrune \cite{graspune-acl2026} learn global budget-constrained gates but treat decisions as static across generation.

\paragraph{Interaction Index.}
For a text segment, let $\text{PPL}_c$ denote perplexity under condition $c \in \{d, a, k, b\}$ (dense, attention-only, KV-only, compound). Normalizing by the dense baseline yields $r_c = \text{PPL}_c / \text{PPL}_d$, and:
\begin{equation}
    \mathrm{II} = r_b - r_a - r_k + 1.
    \label{eq:ii}
\end{equation}
$\mathrm{II} > 0$ indicates super-additive degradation; $\mathrm{II} = 0$ indicates additive independence. We adopt the additive null as primary: it assumes $r_b = r_a + r_k - 1$ (compound loss equals sum of individual losses), the most conservative independence assumption. Although the two axes are causally coupled (\S\ref{sec:analysis}), rejection ($\mathrm{II} > 0$) establishes super-additivity regardless of null specification. Appendix~\ref{app:multiplicative-null} reports parallel results under the multiplicative null ($r_b = r_a \cdot r_k$); the phase-severity gradient is preserved ($G_{\times}{=}21$--$64{\times}$), confirming null-choice invariance. The II distribution is heavily right-skewed; we report medians throughout.

\paragraph{Severity gradient.}
To capture phase variation, the per-problem \emph{amplification ratio} is:
\begin{equation}
    G^{(i)} = \frac{\mathrm{II}^{(i)}_{\,\textit{low}}}{\mathrm{II}^{(i)}_{\,\textit{high}}},
    \label{eq:gradient}
\end{equation}
where $\mathrm{II}^{(i)}_{\,\textit{low/high}}$ is the interaction index in the low/high-sparsity phase for problem $i$. We report $G = \widetilde{G^{(i)}}$, the median across problems, with bootstrap CIs. We use the median of per-problem ratios (not the ratio of medians) to preserve per-problem pairing; see Appendix~\ref{app:profiling-subset} for comparison. $G \gg 1$ indicates phase-concentrated severity.

\paragraph{Phase segmentation.}
\label{par:phase-segmentation}
Inspired by ThinKV's phase-aware perspective on reasoning-time compression~\cite{thinkv-iclr2026}, each generated sequence is partitioned into three phases (low-, mid-, high-sparsity) by tercile boundaries of per-position attention sparsity, using a quantile-based segmentation rather than explicit reasoning delimiters (e.g., \texttt{<think>} tokens). Low-sparsity phases correspond to broadly distributed attention (exploratory reasoning); high-sparsity phases to concentrated attention (structured computation). We note that this segmentation is an \emph{operationalization choice}: attention sparsity serves as a continuous proxy for reasoning phase rather than a theoretically grounded boundary. We validate robustness to segmentation granularity (tercile/quartile/quintile; Appendix~\ref{app:seg-robust}) and minimum phase length (Appendix~\ref{app:mpl-sensitivity}). Validation against DeepSeek's explicit \texttt{<think>} markers yields Cohen's $\kappa{=}0.887$ ($n{=}48$; Appendix~\ref{app:think-validation}). Details in Appendix~\ref{app:formulation-details}.

\section{Importance Discrimination Collapse under Compound Sparsity}
\label{sec:analysis}

The mechanisms discussed here are \emph{correlational diagnostics} that illuminate but do not fully explain the $15$--$17{\times}$ severity divergence between strategies. Both strategies exhibit near-identical Gini drops ($<10\%$ difference), yet severity diverges by $15$--$17{\times}$---an order-of-magnitude mismatch indicating that aggregate concentration collapse alone is insufficient. The critical differentiator is \emph{structural specificity} of importance-based pruning, which systematically removes high-attention-score heads (\S\ref{sec:discrimination-collapse-main}).

\subsection{Importance Discrimination Collapse}
\label{sec:discrimination-collapse-main}

Head pruning systematically reduces the discriminative power of cumulative importance scores, suggesting a mechanism for the phase-strategy interaction. The Gini coefficient decreases by $0.030$--$0.081$ (DeepSeek, $6$--$17\%$ relative to dense baseline) and $0.010$--$0.020$ (Qwen, $2$--$4\%$) after pruning, with consistent negative deltas in all 16 conditions (Appendix~\ref{app:analysis}, Table~\ref{tab:discrimination-metrics-full}). Random eviction shows near-identical Gini drops (e.g., DeepSeek $s{=}0.25$: $-0.065$ vs.\ $-0.060$), confirming that collapse is a consequence of head pruning \emph{per se}. Yet importance-based severity is $15$--$17{\times}$ larger---the amplification arises because importance-based eviction \emph{depends on} the very scores that pruning degrades.

This pattern---\emph{importance discrimination collapse}---reflects eviction degenerating into noise-sensitive selection as score distributions approach uniformity. The effect is phase-dependent: low-sparsity phases have broadly distributed attention where collapse has maximum impact; high-sparsity phases preserve concentration. \cite{hies-2025} report this collapse under single-axis pruning; we extend it to the compound setting.

To provide quantitative evidence for structural specificity, we analyzed the attention Gini coefficients of importance-pruned versus retained heads. Importance pruning (Frobenius norm criterion) systematically removes heads with lower Gini coefficients---i.e., heads with more dispersed attention patterns (Mann-Whitney $U$, $p < 10^{-28}$; rank-biserial $r = 0.62$--$0.76$ across all 8 conditions). Random pruning, by contrast, samples heads with Gini indistinguishable from the population mean (200-trial verification). The Frobenius norm and Gini share sensitivity to attention magnitude; the large effect size ($r > 0.6$) nonetheless indicates strong structural separation.

We note that this evidence is correlational; causal validation through controlled manipulation of importance-score entropy is an important direction for future work.

\subsection{Entropy Redistribution}
\label{sec:entropy-redistribution-main}

As a supplementary observation, head pruning also redistributes the attention entropy of surviving heads on DeepSeek ($\Delta\mathcal{H} \in [-0.047, -0.040]$; Appendix~\ref{app:analysis}, Table~\ref{tab:entropy-deltas}). However, entropy redistribution shows model-dependent behavior---the direction reverses on Qwen at $s{=}0.25$ ($\Delta\mathcal{H} = +0.045$)---replicating in only 2/4 Qwen conditions and thus only partially consistent. Importance discrimination collapse remains the more robust, model-independent correlate, though it partially accounts for rather than fully explains the severity divergence.

\section{Downstream Accuracy Validation Details}
\label{app:accuracy-details}

Table~\ref{tab:accuracy} reports GSM8K accuracy under compound sparsity (importance-based eviction) at $s \in \{0.15, 0.20, 0.25, 0.30\}$, complementing the PPL-based analysis in \S\ref{sec:accuracy-validation}. All conditions use identical generation parameters (\texttt{max\_new\_tokens}${=}$2048). Qwen provides the primary dose-response evidence (truncation rate $\leq 10\%$): accuracy is tolerant through $s{=}0.25$ ($33$--$36\%$ vs.\ $40\%$ dense, all within ${\sim}7$\,pp; individual differences non-significant under binomial CIs) before a sharp cliff at $s{=}0.30$ ($14\%$, $n{=}50$). DeepSeek's dense baseline is constrained by the token limit ($56\%$ truncation rate), so its accuracy is reported at $s \in \{0.15, 0.30\}$ only as directional confirmation.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{GSM8K accuracy under compound sparsity (importance-based eviction). $\delta$: accuracy change vs.\ dense. DS: $n{=}50$; QW: $n{=}100$ (dense, $s{=}0.15$--$0.25$). $^{\dagger}$$s{=}0.30$ uses $n{=}50$ sparse accuracy; $n{=}100$ pending.}
\label{tab:accuracy}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}
\toprule
Model & $s$ & Dense & Sparse & $\delta$ \\
\midrule
DS & 0.15 & 52\% & 34\% & $-$18\,pp \\
DS & 0.30 & 52\% & 10\% & $-$42\,pp \\
\addlinespace
QW & 0.15 & 40\% & 36\% & $-$4\,pp \\
QW & 0.20 & 40\% & 35\% & $-$5\,pp \\
QW & 0.25 & 40\% & 33\% & $-$7\,pp \\
QW & 0.30 & 40\% & 14\% & $-$26\,pp$^{\dagger}$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Failure mode divergence.}
DeepSeek and Qwen exhibit qualitatively distinct failure modes under compound sparsity. DeepSeek failures are \emph{truncation-dominated}: compound sparsity induces verbose, repetitive chain-of-thought that exceeds the token limit before generating an answer (truncation rate rises from 52\% at $s{=}0.15$ to 66\% at $s{=}0.30$). Qwen failures are \emph{wrong-answer-dominated}: outputs complete within the token limit (truncation rate $\leq 8\%$) but produce incorrect answers, suggesting that compound sparsity disrupts reasoning quality rather than reasoning length. This divergence likely reflects training-paradigm differences: DeepSeek's distillation from DeepSeek-R1 produces extended CoT traces that are more susceptible to truncation under degraded conditions.

\paragraph{Language switching under high sparsity.}
At $s{=}0.30$, 40\% of DeepSeek problems exhibit language switching to Chinese mid-generation (dense: 0\%; $s{=}0.15$: 4\%). This qualitative pathology---absent in Qwen---suggests that high-dose compound sparsity can disrupt language-level coherence in distillation-trained models, consistent with the model's Chinese-language pretraining corpus surfacing under degraded attention.


\section{Non-Truncated Sensitivity Analysis}
\label{app:accuracy-nt}

To disentangle truncation artifacts from genuine reasoning degradation, we restrict the accuracy analysis to \emph{non-truncated} problems---those where the model completes generation before hitting the token limit under all conditions (dense, $s{=}0.15$, $s{=}0.30$).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{Non-truncated (NT) accuracy on GSM8K. $n_{\text{NT}}$: problems completing generation under all conditions. $\delta$: accuracy change vs.\ dense NT subset.}
\label{tab:accuracy-nt}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}
\toprule
Model & $s$ & $n_{\text{NT}}$ & Dense & Sparse & $\delta$ \\
\midrule
QW & 0.15 & 42 & 33\% & 33\% & 0\,pp \\
QW & 0.30 & 42 & 33\% & 12\% & $-$21\,pp \\
\addlinespace
DS & 0.15 & 17 & 94\% & 76\% & $-$18\,pp \\
DS & 0.30 & 9 & 100\% & 44\% & $-$56\,pp \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Qwen retains $n_{\text{NT}}{=}42/50$ problems (minimal selection bias), confirming that the dose-dependent accuracy drop ($0$\,pp at $s{=}0.15$, $-21$\,pp at $s{=}0.30$) reflects genuine reasoning quality degradation rather than truncation artifacts.

DeepSeek retains only $n_{\text{NT}}{=}17$ at $s{=}0.15$ and $9$ at $s{=}0.30$, introducing substantial selection bias: the surviving subset consists of easy problems that the dense model solves within the token limit (dense accuracy $94$--$100\%$, vs.\ $52\%$ population-level). Despite this inflated baseline, compound sparsity still degrades accuracy by $18$--$56$\,pp on this easy-problem subset, directionally confirming the Qwen finding. However, DeepSeek NT absolute values should not be compared directly with the full-sample analysis due to the non-representative subsample.

Across both models, 93.2\% of answer tokens (\verb|\boxed{}| markers) fall within the high-sparsity phase, where compound interaction is minimal ($G \approx 1$). This non-uniformity identifies \emph{where} severity concentrates: reasoning-trace tokens show $5.7$--$43.5{\times}$ PPL degradation vs.\ $1.3$--$5.3{\times}$ for answer tokens (Wilcoxon $p < 0.001$). However, reasoning-trace degradation may produce \emph{silent reasoning errors}---correct formatting with corrupted intermediate logic---a failure mode that PPL detects but answer-level accuracy may miss.

\subsection{Downstream Accuracy Validation}
\label{sec:accuracy-validation}

To validate that the PPL-based severity gradient translates to downstream task performance, we evaluate GSM8K accuracy (importance-based eviction, \texttt{max\_new\_tokens}${=}$2048).
Qwen provides the cleanest estimate (truncation rate $\leq 10\%$; $n{=}100$): accuracy is tolerant through $s{=}0.25$ ($33$--$36\%$ vs.\ $40\%$ dense, all within ${\sim}7$\,pp) before a sharp cliff at $s{=}0.30$ ($14\%$, $n{=}50$). Individual sparsity-level differences at $s \leq 0.25$ fall within binomial CIs ($\pm 9$\,pp) and are not statistically significant; the primary finding is the threshold between tolerance ($s \leq 0.25$) and collapse ($s{=}0.30$). DeepSeek shows directionally consistent degradation.\footnote{DeepSeek accuracy deltas include a truncation confound: the dense baseline itself suffers $56\%$ truncation under \texttt{max\_new\_tokens}${=}$2048. The Qwen comparison (truncation $\leq 10\%$) provides a cleaner estimate.}

The two models exhibit distinct failure modes: DeepSeek failures are \emph{truncation-dominated}---compound sparsity induces verbose CoT that exceeds the token limit---while Qwen failures are \emph{wrong-answer-dominated}---outputs complete but produce incorrect results. This divergence suggests that compound sparsity's degradation pathway is training-paradigm-dependent (Appendix~\ref{app:accuracy-details}).
Even restricting to non-truncated problems where models complete their reasoning chains ($n_{\text{NT}}{=}42/50$ for Qwen), compound sparsity at $s{=}0.30$ degrades accuracy by $21$\,pp ($\delta{=}0$\,pp at $s{=}0.15$), confirming that the observed PPL increases correspond to genuine reasoning quality degradation rather than truncation artifacts (Appendix~\ref{app:accuracy-nt}).

We provide a correlational mechanistic analysis in terms of importance discrimination collapse in \S\ref{sec:analysis}.

